.TH PHASORSTD_HTTP 3 "August 2026" "Phasor 4.0.0" "Phasor Standard Library 4.0.0"
.SH NAME
phasorstd_http \- Phasor HTTP client functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBinclude "std/http";\fR
.PP
.B http_request(method, url[, body[, headers[, timeoutMs[, maxRedirects]]]])
.B http_useragent(user_agent)
.PP
.I "Convenience wrappers, all built on http_request():"
.B http_get(url, headers, timeoutMs)
.B http_post(url, body, headers, timeoutMs)
.B http_put(url, body, headers, timeoutMs)
.B http_patch(url, body, headers, timeoutMs)
.B http_delete(url, body, headers, timeoutMs)
.B http_head(url, headers, timeoutMs)
.B http_options(url, headers, timeoutMs)
.fi
.SH TYPES
.TP
.B HTTPHeader
.RS
.nf
struct HTTPHeader {
    key: string,
    value: string
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B HTTPResponse
.RS
.nf
struct HTTPResponse {
    status: int,
    headers: HTTPHeader[],
    body: string
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B HTTPCode
A struct of named integer constants for common HTTP status codes, e.g.\&
\fBHTTPCode.OK\fR (200), \fBHTTPCode.NotFound\fR (404), \fBHTTPCode.InternalServerError\fR
(500). Available once \fBstd/http\fR (or \fBstd/net.phs\fR) is included.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BR http_request (method, url[, body[, headers[, timeoutMs[, maxRedirects]]]])
Perform an HTTP request. This is the underlying function every convenience wrapper below
calls; use it directly for methods or options the wrappers don't expose.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBmethod\fR 12
HTTP method, e.g.\& \fB"GET"\fR, \fB"POST"\fR, \fB"PUT"\fR, \fB"DELETE"\fR
.IP \fBurl\fR 12
Full URL, including scheme (\fBhttp://\fR only \(em see NOTES) and host
.IP \fBbody\fR 12
Optional request body string. Pass null to send no body.
.IP \fBheaders\fR 12
Optional array of \fBHTTPHeader\fR structs (or equivalent \fB{key, value}\fR structs) to add
to the request. A \fBHost\fR, \fBConnection\fR, \fBUser-Agent\fR, and \fBAccept\fR header are
always added automatically; a \fBContent-Length\fR header is added automatically for a
non-empty \fBbody\fR unless one of your headers already sets it (case-insensitively).
.IP \fBtimeoutMs\fR 12
Optional connect/read/write timeout in milliseconds (default \fB15000\fR)
.IP \fBmaxRedirects\fR 12
Optional maximum number of \fB3xx\fR redirects to follow (default \fB5\fR). A \fB301\fR,
\fB302\fR, or \fB303\fR redirect switches the method to \fBGET\fR and drops the body, matching
common browser behavior; other \fB3xx\fR statuses with a \fBLocation\fR header re-request the
new location with the same method and body.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
An \fBHTTPResponse\fR struct: \fBstatus\fR (integer status code, or \fB0\fR if the final
response's status line couldn't be parsed), \fBheaders\fR (array of \fBHTTPHeader\fR structs
from the final response), and \fBbody\fR (the final response's body, with \fBchunked\fR
transfer-encoding already decoded)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBurl\fR cannot be parsed, if \fBurl\fR (or a redirect target) uses a scheme
other than \fBhttp\fR (including \fBhttps\fR \(em see NOTES), or if the connection to the
server fails
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR http_useragent (user_agent)
Set the \fBUser-Agent\fR header sent with every subsequent \fBhttp_request()\fR call (and
therefore every convenience wrapper call).
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBuser_agent\fR 12
New User-Agent string, replacing the default entirely
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Null
.RE
.SH CONVENIENCE WRAPPERS
Each of these simply calls \fBhttp_request()\fR, so their argument and
return semantics are otherwise identical to it. All of them use the default
\fBmaxRedirects\fR (5); use \fBhttp_request()\fR directly if you need to change it!
.TP
.BR http_get (url, headers, timeoutMs)
Equivalent to \fBhttp_request("GET", url, null, headers, timeoutMs)\fR.
.TP
.BR http_post (url, body, headers, timeoutMs)
Equivalent to \fBhttp_request("POST", url, body, headers, timeoutMs)\fR.
.TP
.BR http_put (url, body, headers, timeoutMs)
Equivalent to \fBhttp_request("PUT", url, body, headers, timeoutMs)\fR.
.TP
.BR http_patch (url, body, headers, timeoutMs)
Equivalent to \fBhttp_request("PATCH", url, body, headers, timeoutMs)\fR.
.TP
.BR http_delete (url, body, headers, timeoutMs)
Equivalent to \fBhttp_request("DELETE", url, body, headers, timeoutMs)\fR.
.TP
.BR http_head (url, headers, timeoutMs)
Equivalent to \fBhttp_request("HEAD", url, null, headers, timeoutMs)\fR.
.TP
.BR http_options (url, headers, timeoutMs)
Equivalent to \fBhttp_request("OPTIONS", url, null, headers, timeoutMs)\fR.
.PP
Unlike \fBhttp_request()\fR, none of these seven wrappers treat \fBheaders\fR or
\fBtimeoutMs\fR as optional at the Phasor call-site \(em pass an empty array (\fB[]\fR) for
no extra headers, and the default timeout explicitly if you want it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Simple GET
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/http";

var res: HTTPResponse = http_get("http://example.com/api/status", [], 10000);
if (res.status == HTTPCode.OK) {
    puts(res.body);
} else {
    putf("Request failed with status %d", res.status);
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B POST with Headers
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/http";

var headers: HTTPHeader[] = [
    { key: "Content-Type", value: "application/json" },
    { key: "Authorization", value: "Bearer some-token" }
];

var res: HTTPResponse = http_post(
    "http://example.com/api/items",
    "{\e"name\e": \e"widget\e"}",
    headers,
    10000
);

putf("Status: %d", res.status);
for (var i: i64 = 0; i < res.headers.length; i++) {
    putf("%s: %s", res.headers[i].key, res.headers[i].value);
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Custom User-Agent and Raw Request
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/http";

http_useragent("MyPhasorApp/1.0");

// http_request() directly, to control redirect following
var res: HTTPResponse = http_request("GET", "http://example.com/", null, [], 5000, 0);
puts(res.status);  // a 3xx status here, since redirects are disabled (maxRedirects: 0)
.RE
.fi
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
.B https://
URLs are not supported: TLS/SSL is not implemented, and \fBhttp_request()\fR throws a runtime
error if given (or redirected to) an \fBhttps://\fR URL. Only plain \fBhttp://\fR is usable for now.
.IP \(bu 2
.B http_useragent()
changes the User-Agent for the whole process going forward, not just the next call; there is
no way to override it for a single request without changing it back afterward
.IP \(bu 2
Each call to \fBhttp_request()\fR (and therefore each wrapper call) opens and closes its own
TCP connection; there is no connection reuse or keep-alive across calls
.IP \(bu 2
A response with no \fBContent-Length\fR and no chunked \fBTransfer-Encoding\fR is read until
the server closes the connection, which relies on the request having sent
\fBConnection: close\fR (always true here, since that header is added automatically)
